<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">German reunification</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Reunification of Germany</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">German reunification was the process by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> (East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>) joined the Federal Republic of Germany (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>) on 3 October 1990, ending the division of the country that had lasted since 1949. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> and East Germany's first free elections in March 1990, the two states formed a monetary and economic union, and the Two Plus Four Treaty with the four wartime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> restored full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to a united Germany. The date is celebrated as German Unity Day.</p></div>
